==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|rites of passage}}

# {{lb|en|anthropology}} A [[ceremony]] or series of ceremonies, often very [[ritualized]], to [[celebrate]] a [[transition]] in a person’s life. [[baptism|Baptisms]], [[bar mitzvah]]s, [[wedding]]s and [[funeral]]s are among the best known examples.
#: {{ux|en|After John officially attained his majority, Robert bought him his first legal beer. This is a common American '''rite of passage'''.}}
#*{{quote-web|en|September 15 2021|author=Laura Martin|url=https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20210914-how-talent-shows-became-tvs-most-bizarre-programmes|work=BBC|title=How talent shows became TV's most bizarre programmes|passage=Not only did these holiday resorts become a '''rite of passage''' for TV's biggest talents – Red Coats comedians, magicians and presenters were often scouted, then sent to perform live at places like the London Palladium, before securing a television contract – but the culture within them meant that the audience were already invested in this type of entertainment once they returned home.}}

